          {
            "number": 7,
            "text": "Which is not another; but there be some that trouble you, and would pervert the gospel of Christ."
          },
          {
            "number": 8,
            "text": "But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach any other gospel unto you than that which we have preached unto you, let him be accursed."
          },
          {
            "number": 9,
            "text": "As we said before, so say I now again, If any man preach any other gospel unto you than that ye have received, let him be accursed."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ephesians-2-8-10",
        "label": "Ephesians 2:8–10",
        "context": "An evangelical response emphasizes salvation as God’s gift and good works as its result. Ask what the missionary means by salvation and exaltation before assuming identical definitions.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 8,
            "text": "For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God:"
          },
          {
            "number": 9,
            "text": "Not of works, lest any man should boast."
          },
          {
            "number": 10,
            "text": "For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "deuteronomy-6-4",
        "label": "Deuteronomy 6:4",
        "context": "Israel’s confession of one God. The Trinitarian case reads this together with passages about the Father, Son, and Spirit; this verse alone does not articulate the whole doctrine.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 4,
            "text": "Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God is one LORD:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "isaiah-43-10",
        "label": "Isaiah 43:10",
        "context": "God contrasts himself with rival gods. An evangelical response asks whether unity of purpose among separate divine beings sufficiently accounts for this claim.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 10,
            "text": "Ye are my witnesses, saith the LORD, and my servant whom I have chosen: that ye may know and believe me, and understand that I am he: before me there was no God formed, neither shall there be after me."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "john-20-27-29",
        "label": "John 20:27–29",
        "context": "Thomas addresses the risen Jesus as Lord and God. Trinitarians connect this with John’s opening description of the Word, while distinguishing the Son from the Father.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 27,
            "text": "Then saith he to Thomas, Reach hither thy finger, and behold my hands; and reach hither thy hand, and thrust it into my side: and be not faithless, but believing."
          },
          {
            "number": 28,
            "text": "And Thomas answered and said unto him, My Lord and my God."
          },
          {
            "number": 29,
            "text": "Jesus saith unto him, Thomas, because thou hast seen me, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed."
