            "number": 4,
            "text": "And whither I go ye know, and the way ye know."
          },
          {
            "number": 5,
            "text": "Thomas saith unto him, Lord, we know not whither thou goest; and how can we know the way?"
          },
          {
            "number": 6,
            "text": "Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ephesians-4-1-6",
        "label": "Ephesians 4:1–6",
        "context": "Paul urges existing believers to walk in humility and preserve unity. The Christian objection asks whether this passage establishes a later loss and restoration of authority.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 1,
            "text": "I therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you that ye walk worthy of the vocation wherewith ye are called,"
          },
          {
            "number": 2,
            "text": "With all lowliness and meekness, with longsuffering, forbearing one another in love;"
          },
          {
            "number": 3,
            "text": "Endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace."
          },
          {
            "number": 4,
            "text": "There is one body, and one Spirit, even as ye are called in one hope of your calling;"
          },
          {
            "number": 5,
            "text": "One Lord, one faith, one baptism,"
          },
          {
            "number": 6,
            "text": "One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in you all."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "james-1-2-6",
        "label": "James 1:2–6",
        "context": "James connects trials, patience, maturity, and asking God for wisdom. Prayer matters; the passage alone does not authenticate a particular vision or prophet.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 2,
            "text": "My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations;"
          },
          {
            "number": 3,
            "text": "Knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience."
          },
          {
            "number": 4,
            "text": "But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing."
          },
          {
            "number": 5,
            "text": "If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him."
          },
          {
            "number": 6,
            "text": "But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "john-17-1-5",
        "label": "John 17:1–5",
        "context": "Jesus addresses the Father as distinct from himself and speaks of glory shared before creation. Trinitarians read this together with John 1, not as a denial of the distinction between Father and Son.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 1,
            "text": "These words spake Jesus, and lifted up his eyes to heaven, and said, Father, the hour is come; glorify thy Son, that thy Son also may glorify thee:"
          },
          {
            "number": 2,
            "text": "As thou hast given him power over all flesh, that he should give eternal life to as many as thou hast given him."
          },
          {
            "number": 3,
            "text": "And this is life eternal, that they might know thee the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom thou hast sent."
          },
          {
            "number": 4,
            "text": "I have glorified thee on the earth: I have finished the work which thou gavest me to do."
          },
          {
            "number": 5,
            "text": "And now, O Father, glorify thou me with thine own self with the glory which I had with thee before the world was."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "john-17-20-23",
        "label": "John 17:20–23",
        "context": "Jesus prays for the unity of future believers. The debate concerns what this analogy establishes about divine oneness; neither side should make this paragraph carry its entire doctrine.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 20,
            "text": "Neither pray I for these alone, but for them also which shall believe on me through their word;"
          },
          {
            "number": 21,
            "text": "That they all may be one; as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee, that they also may be one in us: that the world may believe that thou hast sent me."
          },
          {
            "number": 22,
            "text": "And the glory which thou gavest me I have given them; that they may be one, even as we are one:"
          },
          {
            "number": 23,
            "text": "I in them, and thou in me, that they may be made perfect in one; and that the world may know that thou hast sent me, and hast loved them, as thou hast loved me."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2-corinthians-13-1-3",
        "label": "2 Corinthians 13:1–3",
        "context": "Paul discusses his coming visit and discipline concerning sin. LDS teaching applies the witness principle to two books; that application is an interpretive step, not an explicit identification of the Book of Mormon in this text.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 1,
            "text": "This is the third time I am coming to you. In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word be established."
          },
          {
            "number": 2,
            "text": "I told you before, and foretell you, as if I were present, the second time; and being absent now I write to them which heretofore have sinned, and to all other, that, if I come again, I will not spare:"
          },
          {
            "number": 3,
            "text": "Since ye seek a proof of Christ speaking in me, which to you-ward is not weak, but is mighty in you."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "1-corinthians-15-35-42",
        "label": "1 Corinthians 15:35–42",
        "context": "Paul asks what kind of body the raised dead receive, then compares earthly and heavenly bodies and differing glories. Evangelical readers argue this does not by itself establish the three LDS destinations; LDS readers connect it with later revelation.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 35,
            "text": "But some man will say, How are the dead raised up? and with what body do they come?"
          },
          {
            "number": 36,
            "text": "Thou fool, that which thou sowest is not quickened, except it die:"
          },
          {
            "number": 37,
            "text": "And that which thou sowest, thou sowest not that body that shall be, but bare grain, it may chance of wheat, or of some other grain:"
          },
          {
            "number": 38,
            "text": "But God giveth it a body as it hath pleased him, and to every seed his own body."
          },
          {
            "number": 39,
            "text": "All flesh is not the same flesh: but there is one kind of flesh of men, another flesh of beasts, another of fishes, and another of birds."
          },
          {
            "number": 40,
            "text": "There are also celestial bodies, and bodies terrestrial: but the glory of the celestial is one, and the glory of the terrestrial is another."
          },
          {
            "number": 41,
            "text": "There is one glory of the sun, and another glory of the moon, and another glory of the stars: for one star differeth from another star in glory."
          },
          {
            "number": 42,
            "text": "So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in corruption; it is raised in incorruption:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "john-1-1-3",
        "label": "John 1:1–3",
        "context": "The Word is distinguished from God and called God. Christian apologetics reads this alongside passages affirming one God and the personal distinction of Father and Son.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 1,
            "text": "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God."
          },
          {
            "number": 2,
            "text": "The same was in the beginning with God."
          },
          {
            "number": 3,
            "text": "All things were made by him; and without him was not any thing made that was made."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "matthew-16-18",
        "label": "Matthew 16:18",
        "context": "Christians commonly appeal to this promise when questioning a total loss of the Church. Traditions differ on the rock and what the promise entails; it is not a full historical argument by itself.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 18,
            "text": "And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "galatians-1-6-9",
        "label": "Galatians 1:6–9",
        "context": "Paul tells the Galatians to evaluate even impressive messengers by the gospel already preached. This supplies a test; a claimed contradiction must still be demonstrated.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 6,
            "text": "I marvel that ye are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Christ unto another gospel:"
          },
          {
            "number": 7,
            "text": "Which is not another; but there be some that trouble you, and would pervert the gospel of Christ."
          },
          {
            "number": 8,
            "text": "But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach any other gospel unto you than that which we have preached unto you, let him be accursed."
          },
          {
            "number": 9,
            "text": "As we said before, so say I now again, If any man preach any other gospel unto you than that ye have received, let him be accursed."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ephesians-2-8-10",
        "label": "Ephesians 2:8–10",
        "context": "An evangelical response emphasizes salvation as God’s gift and good works as its result. Ask what the missionary means by salvation and exaltation before assuming identical definitions.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 8,
            "text": "For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God:"
          },
          {
            "number": 9,
            "text": "Not of works, lest any man should boast."
          },
          {
            "number": 10,
            "text": "For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "deuteronomy-6-4",
        "label": "Deuteronomy 6:4",
        "context": "Israel’s confession of one God. The Trinitarian case reads this together with passages about the Father, Son, and Spirit; this verse alone does not articulate the whole doctrine.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 4,
            "text": "Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God is one LORD:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "isaiah-43-10",
        "label": "Isaiah 43:10",
        "context": "God contrasts himself with rival gods. An evangelical response asks whether unity of purpose among separate divine beings sufficiently accounts for this claim.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 10,
            "text": "Ye are my witnesses, saith the LORD, and my servant whom I have chosen: that ye may know and believe me, and understand that I am he: before me there was no God formed, neither shall there be after me."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "john-20-27-29",
        "label": "John 20:27–29",
        "context": "Thomas addresses the risen Jesus as Lord and God. Trinitarians connect this with John’s opening description of the Word, while distinguishing the Son from the Father.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 27,
            "text": "Then saith he to Thomas, Reach hither thy finger, and behold my hands; and reach hither thy hand, and thrust it into my side: and be not faithless, but believing."
          },
          {
            "number": 28,
            "text": "And Thomas answered and said unto him, My Lord and my God."
          },
          {
            "number": 29,
            "text": "Jesus saith unto him, Thomas, because thou hast seen me, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "acts-5-3-4",
        "label": "Acts 5:3–4",
        "context": "Peter describes lying to the Holy Ghost as lying to God. This is one part of the evangelical case for the Spirit’s divinity, alongside monotheism and personal distinctions.",
        "verses": [
          {
            "number": 3,
            "text": "But Peter said, Ananias, why hath Satan filled thine heart to lie to the Holy Ghost, and to keep back part of the price of the land?"
          },
          {
            "number": 4,
            "text": "Whiles it remained, was it not thine own? and after it was sold, was it not in thine own power? why hast thou conceived this thing in thine heart? thou hast not lied unto men, but unto God."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "sourceNotes": "Extracted verbatim from the approved prototype (doorstep-prototype.html, version 24, AI developer guide of 21 October 2026). Christian responses represent the authored evangelical apologetic perspective. Quotations are KJV; each quoted phrase links to its supplied passage range. Weekly reading links point to Watchman Fellowship."
